<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · other</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valladolid debate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1550–1551</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Valladolid Controversy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Valladolid debate was a formal dispute held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1550–1551 on the treatment of the indigenous peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a> defended their rights and humanity, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-gines-de-sepulveda">Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda</a> argued that their conquest and subjugation were justified. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> discuss it in the context of colonial governance and the moral questions raised by conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valladolid-debate.jpg" alt="Valladolid debate" width="316" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Queninosta · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Gregorio_VA_(6).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
